+# hb_program -- the daemon, verbatim. Quoted heredoc: nothing in it is expanded by bash.
+hb_program() {
+    cat <<'NDTWIN_LAB_HEARTBEAT_PY'
+"""ndtwin-lab heartbeat -- the veth heartbeat of a foreign P4 fabric (TICKET-P4-heartbeat, segment H).
+
+[Co-developed with claude code -- Adam]
+
+WHAT THIS IS. `sudo ndtwin-lab heartbeat start` writes this program to /run/ndtwin-lab/heartbeat.py
+and runs it as root. Once per PERIOD_S it sends one small frame out of every switch-side veth that
+has a switch on its far end, and it listens on every switch-side veth of the running fabric. It
+writes down, per direction, WHEN it last heard that direction's frame arrive at the far end. That
+is all it does. It decides nothing: whether a direction is down, and what to do about it, is the
+proxy's business (segment W), with the proxy's own constants.
+
+WHY A veth HEARTBEAT (doc/audit/2026-09-24_p4-driven-api-shape/ANALYSIS.md section 5). A foreign
+pipeline carries no NDTwin LLDP, so nothing tells the proxy a link died. `tc netem` sits on the
+EGRESS qdisc of a switch-side veth, and a frame sent into that veth with AF_PACKET goes through the
+same qdisc -- so when a link is cut the heartbeat is cut with it, and when it is not the frame
+arrives on the peer veth, where this program reads it before (and independently of) the switch.
+
+🔴 WHAT IT WILL NOT TAKE FROM ANYBODY. It runs as root and it accepts exactly one word on its
+command line (plan | daemon | report). Interface names, paths and the ethertype are never read from
+argv or the environment:
+  * the interfaces come from the RUNNING FABRIC'S OWN DECLARATION -- /tmp/ndtwin_p4_switches.json,
+    written by the root topology process that built the fabric, and trusted only if it is a
+    regular root-owned file that nobody else can write, in a directory nobody else can replace it
+    in (the same rule tools/p4_power_helper.py applies before it executes that file's argv);
+  * each switch's interfaces are the `-i <port>@<ifname>` pairs bmv2 was launched with, and every
+    one must be named `<switch>-eth<port>`, exist, and be driven by `veth`;
+  * a LINK is two such interfaces that are each other's veth peer, read from the kernel (both
+    `iflink`s must point at each other). An interface whose peer is not in the fabric is
+    host-facing: it is listened on and NEVER sent on.
+
+WHAT "HEARD" MEANS, and the two ways it could lie, each closed here with a test:
+  * our frame leaving an interface is visible on that interface too (PACKET_OUTGOING). A daemon
+    that counted it would hear a direction whose far end has been cut. Only an INCOMING frame is
+    evidence, and only on the exact interface its direction names;
+  * a switch's pipeline may forward our frame on. Arriving somewhere else it is `misdelivered`;
+    leaving a switch port it is `forwarded` -- counted, disclosed, never heard.
+"""
